From: ViKing <viking.yfy@qq.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 13:34:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath11k: own RX buffers with page pools and indexed lookup

The RX refill ring takes every buffer from dev_alloc_skb(), maps it with
dma_map_single() and unmaps it again when the frame is reaped. Buffers of
the ring share the per-CPU page-frag caches with unrelated allocations, so
one long-lived skb pins a whole high-order page.

Every RX buffer gets an id from the ring's IDR when it is posted and gives
it back when it is reaped, and the IDR allocation is one of the larger
costs per received frame. The ids a ring hands out are bounded by the ring
size, so keep them in a table indexed by id with a stack of the free ones
instead. Posting a buffer pops an id and reaping it pushes the id back,
both in constant time under the existing ring lock.

Merged from:
  992-13-wifi-ath11k-use-page_pool-for-the-RX-refill-ring.patch
  992-14-wifi-ath11k-replace-the-RX-buffer-IDR-with-a-table.patch

Based on work by:
  Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>

Link: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-wireless/20260719215817.2468580-1-wabsie@gmail.com/
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-wireless/20261008-ath12k-pp-v1-0-68f888cd7eec@oss.qualcomm.com/

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
Signed-off-by: ViKing <viking.yfy@qq.com>

